0..76 Comment "// One program draws a word of four bits, and three models of `std.rand.Eff`"
77..153 Comment "// answer its draws: the host\'s entropy, the seeded generator `std.rand.lcg`"
154..177 Comment "// and the exact model."
178..180 Comment "//"
181..255 Comment "// A generator written in the language answers the same draws as the host."
256..330 Comment "// With a constant seed, each run gives the same draws. So a test sees one"
331..407 Comment "// outcome. Over a uniform seed, the output of the generator is uniform too."
408..410 Comment "//"
411..487 Comment "// `gandr run seeded.gandr` exits with a word from the host\'s entropy. Under"
488..563 Comment "// `lcg 8 0`, the state goes from 0 to 79 and then to 50, so the word is 4."
564..637 Comment "// The exact model runs `word` once for each of its 16 paths, each at the"
638..713 Comment "// weight `1/16`. At the width 8, a step of the generator is a bijection on"
714..790 Comment "// the 256 states. So over a uniform seed of 8 bits, the top two bits of the"
791..832 Comment "// first output take each value at `1/4`."
833..835 Comment "//"
836..902 Comment "// - see: `library/std/src/rand.gandr` — `lcg` and its constants"
903..979 Comment "// - see: `draws.gandr` — the same three models, with a fixed list for the"
980..991 Comment "//   second"
992..1007 Comment "// - see: #5713"
1009..1078 Comment "// The seeded generator, a model of the host interface of randomness."
